{
  "concept": "Kirchhoff's rules turn one multi-loop circuit into solvable equations: at each junction the currents balance, and around any closed loop the signed voltage rises and drops sum to zero — node labels a–f are bookkeeping laid over an unchanged physical circuit.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "single rectangular circuit frame split by a center vertical branch into two loops (the interactive shows one drawing whose caption swaps between (a) and (b) instead of two side-by-side panels)",
    "resistor R1 = 3.00 Ω (blue zigzag) on the top wire of the left loop, between a and b",
    "resistor R3 = 3.00 Ω (blue zigzag) on the top wire of the right loop, between b and c",
    "resistor R2 = 3.00 Ω (blue zigzag) on the shared center branch, between b and e",
    "resistor R4 = 4.00 Ω (blue zigzag) on the right vertical branch, between c and d",
    "plain left vertical wire between a and f",
    "battery V1 = 24.00 V on the bottom wire of the left loop, + terminal on the left",
    "battery V2 = 29.00 V on the bottom wire of the right loop, + terminal on the right",
    "six teal node dots at a, b, c (top) and f, e, d (bottom), visible in labeled mode",
    "node letters a, b, c, d, e, f beside their dots, visible in labeled mode",
    "caption '(a)' or '(b)' beneath the circuit, matching the toggle",
    "current arrows labeled I1, I2, I3 on the three branches meeting at the selected junction (junction mode)",
    "junction equation annotation 'I1 = I2 + I3' (junction mode)",
    "loop-trace traversal highlight that visits each element in order (loop mode)",
    "equation strip accumulating signed voltage terms and ending in 'ΣΔV = 0' (loop mode)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "circuitLabels",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Circuit view",
      "options": ["Unlabeled (a)", "Labeled (b)"],
      "default": "Unlabeled (a)",
      "teaches": "Node dots and letters are analysis scaffolding laid over the same physical circuit — toggling shows that every resistor, battery, and wire is identical; only the naming needed for Kirchhoff's rules is added"
    },
    {
      "id": "junctionClick",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "At a junction the currents in the three connected branches must balance — clicking b or e highlights those branches, draws current arrows, and shows I1 = I2 + I3 (current in equals current out)"
    },
    {
      "id": "loopTrace",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Loop tracing",
      "states": ["idle", "left loop", "right loop"],
      "default": "idle",
      "teaches": "Walking a closed loop in one direction assigns each element a signed term — a rise across a battery traversed − to +, a drop across a resistor traversed with the current (a rise against it) — and the accumulated terms must total zero"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The bare circuit",
      "narration": "Panel (a) shows the physical circuit: two loops sharing the middle branch R2, with V1 = 24.00 V (+ terminal left) in the left loop and V2 = 29.00 V (+ terminal right) in the right loop. Nothing here is labeled yet.",
      "state": { "circuitLabels": "Unlabeled (a)", "loopTrace": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Labels are scaffolding",
      "narration": "Switching to panel (b) adds teal dots and letters a–f at the corners and branch points — but not a single component changes. The labels exist only so Kirchhoff's rules can name places; b and e are the junctions where three wires meet.",
      "state": { "circuitLabels": "Labeled (b)", "loopTrace": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Junction rule",
      "narration": "Click junction b (or e): its three branches highlight, current arrows appear, and the junction equation I1 = I2 + I3 states that whatever current flows in must flow back out.",
      "state": { "circuitLabels": "Labeled (b)", "loopTrace": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Loop rule — left loop",
      "narration": "Trace the left loop a→b→e→f→a: each element lights in traversal order as its term is added — a drop I1R1 across R1, a drop I3R2 down R2, a rise of V1 = 24 V through the battery — building an equation that must sum to zero.",
      "state": { "circuitLabels": "Labeled (b)", "loopTrace": "left loop" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Loop rule — right loop",
      "narration": "Trace the right loop b→c→d→e→b: the shared branch R2 now contributes a term of the opposite sign because the traversal goes against I3, and V2 enters as a drop when crossed from + to − — loop direction is what sets the signs.",
      "state": { "circuitLabels": "Labeled (b)", "loopTrace": "right loop" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The labels a–f change nothing physically — they exist so Kirchhoff's two rules can be written down: currents balance at junctions b and e (I1 = I2 + I3), and signed voltage changes sum to zero around either loop.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Draw one circuit, not two panels: a rectangle split by a center vertical branch into two equal loops. Blue zigzag resistors: R1 and R3 along the top (left and right of center), R2 on the center branch, R4 on the right vertical; left vertical is a bare wire. Battery symbols on the bottom of each loop with long/short plates and +/− marks: V1 + on the left, V2 + on the right. Component labels in italic serif styling exactly as 'R1 = 3.00 Ω', 'V1 = 24.00 V', etc. In labeled mode, small teal dots appear at all six corners/branch points with lowercase italic letters a, b, c across the top and f, e, d across the bottom; the caption beneath swaps from (a) to (b) — fade the dots/letters in rather than cutting. Junction-click mode: highlight the three branches meeting at the clicked junction in amber, overlay colored current arrows (I1 entering b from the left, I2 leaving toward c, I3 downward through R2), and place the equation I1 = I2 + I3 nearby. Loop-trace mode: a moving highlight dot travels the chosen loop clockwise, each resistor/battery flashes amber as reached, and a term chip ('+24.0 V', '−I1·3.00 Ω', …) is appended left-to-right in an equation strip below the circuit, closing with 'ΣΔV = 0' once the loop completes; the shared branch R2's term flips sign between the two loops."
}

