        "loopTrace": "idle"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Loop rule — left loop",
      "narration": "Trace the left loop a→b→e→f→a: each element lights in traversal order as its term is added — a drop I1R1 across R1, a drop I3R2 down R2, a rise of V1 = 24 V through the battery — building an equation that must sum to zero.",
      "state": {
        "circuitLabels": "Labeled (b)",
        "loopTrace": "left loop"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Loop rule — right loop",
      "narration": "Trace the right loop b→c→d→e→b: the shared branch R2 now contributes a term of the opposite sign because the traversal goes against I3, and V2 enters as a drop when crossed from + to − — loop direction is what sets the signs.",
      "state": {
        "circuitLabels": "Labeled (b)",
        "loopTrace": "right loop"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The labels a–f change nothing physically — they exist so Kirchhoff's two rules can be written down: currents balance at junctions b and e (I1 = I2 + I3), and signed voltage changes sum to zero around either loop.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Draw one circuit, not two panels: a rectangle split by a center vertical branch into two equal loops. Blue zigzag resistors: R1 and R3 along the top (left and right of center), R2 on the center branch, R4 on the right vertical; left vertical is a bare wire. Battery symbols on the bottom of each loop with long/short plates and +/− marks: V1 + on the left, V2 + on the right. Component labels in italic serif styling exactly as 'R1 = 3.00 Ω', 'V1 = 24.00 V', etc. In labeled mode, small teal dots appear at all six corners/branch points with lowercase italic letters a, b, c across the top and f, e, d across the bottom; the caption beneath swaps from (a) to (b) — fade the dots/letters in rather than cutting. Junction-click mode: highlight the three branches meeting at the clicked junction in amber, overlay colored current arrows (I1 entering b from the left, I2 leaving toward c, I3 downward through R2), and place the equation I1 = I2 + I3 nearby. Loop-trace mode: a moving highlight dot travels the chosen loop clockwise, each resistor/battery flashes amber as reached, and a term chip ('+24.0 V', '−I1·3.00 Ω', …) is appended left-to-right in an equation strip below the circuit, closing with 'ΣΔV = 0' once the loop completes; the shared branch R2's term flips sign between the two loops."
}

